    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study discusses prior work on safety alignment and fine-tuning, but its own experiments measure animal compassion and moral reasoning, not harmful-request refusal, response-position KL, bypasses, or safety-localized parameters.",
    "gate_note": "The study's own post-training results concern compassion and moral reasoning, a related but different construct from the claim's safety/refusal behavior; record those findings under fits_no_version. The safety-fine-tuning results in the introduction are cited from other work, not tested here. The proposed representational-overlap explanation is not directly measured.",
    "test_reason": "The study post-trains one compassion mid-trained Llama 3.1 8B on helpfulness or coding data. It measures only animal-compassion (ANIMA) and moral-reasoning (MORU) scores, never harmful-request compliance or refusal. It uses Dolly, as Qi et al. did, but the construct is different. Qi et al. and Chen et al. 2025a safety results appear only as cited motivation. No KL, prefill, localization or deepening experiments.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. The authors add that mid-trained values seem encoded more deeply and cross-lingually than coding-SFT reasoning gains. They recommend coding-domain post-training and compassion benchmarks for value preservation, with a caveat about the single model and corpus.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study mid-trains one Llama 3.1 8B model on synthetic animal-compassion data, then compares helpfulness versus coding SFT and GRPO using compassion and moral-reasoning benchmarks. It does not fine-tune safety-aligned models on harmful examples or measure refusal.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The helpfulness-versus-coding difference is reported for ANIMA and English-only MORU, but is effectively absent on multilingual MORU; the Hindi MORU comparison reverses direction. GRPO ANIMA scores are lower than SFT scores, and the GRPO arms use different reward models.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On English ANIMA, Dolly SFT falls below base (35.7% vs 60.2%, d=1.12), while Magicoder SFT does not differ (65.2%, p=0.31). The gap holds on non-English items (22.9% vs 48.6%).",
        "proposed_claim": "Benign helpfulness-domain SFT erodes a mid-trained non-safety value (animal compassion) more than coding-domain SFT, cross-lingually.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r13",
          "r14",
          "r15",
          "r16",
          "r17",
          "r18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Under GRPO, both arms fall below base on ANIMA, and helpfulness falls lower (15.4% vs 30.3%, p=0.010). The domain contrast is confounded with reward-model identity.",
        "proposed_claim": "RL post-training on benign data degrades mid-trained compassion, more so for the helpfulness pipeline.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r4",
          "r5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "The English MORU gap (46.4% vs 71.9%) vanishes on multilingual MORU (52.3% vs 51.2%; GRPO 28.0% vs 25.9%). The authors attribute this to Hindi language-compliance differences.",
        "proposed_claim": "The domain effect on general moral reasoning depends on evaluation language and aggregation.",
